# Testing

Write a new automated test when the change adds or changes observable runtime behavior, fixes a confirmed defect that could recur, or changes a public contract that needs regression coverage.

Do not add a test when direct validation completely proves the requested change. Documentation, comments, formatting, metadata, generated output, and mechanical refactors usually need inspection, generation, linting, compilation, or an existing relevant test instead of a new test.

When a new test is necessary, write the smallest end-to-end test that enters through a public boundary, runs the production path under review, and asserts an outcome visible to a user, caller, operator, or downstream system.

Use real dependencies through that public boundary. Temporary databases, temporary directories, and local servers are valid. Do not replace dependencies with mocks, stubs, spies, test doubles, or recorded responses.

Delete tests for removed behavior. Delete or rewrite tests that would still pass when the protected behavior is broken. Do not add static content checks, fixture-only snapshots, or tests that duplicate compiler and linter guarantees.
